@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+Briefcase now provides more helpful error messages when the `SSL_CERT_FILE`, `SSL_CERT_DIR`, or proxy environment variables are misconfigured. The HTTP and HTTPS environment variables honored by Briefcase are also now documented.
diff --git a/docs/en/reference/environment.md b/docs/en/reference/environment.md
index 0c8522c..0169e06 100644
--- a/docs/en/reference/environment.md
+++ b/docs/en/reference/environment.md
@@ -31,3 +31,21 @@ It is *highly* inadvisable to generate production apps while this environment va
 Both macOS and Windows provide mechanisms for running x86_64 binaries on ARM64 hardware. These tools can make it very difficult to generate a packaged application, as it can be difficult to ensure a packaged application contains code matching the right architecture when the platform provides misleading answers about the current platform. As a result, Briefcase will raise an error if it detects that the version of Python that is being used doesn't match the underlying hardware architecture.
 
 However, it is possible to disable this check by setting the `BRIEFCASE_ALLOW_EMULATION` environment variable. This will cause Briefcase to output a warning if it appears that Python is running in emulation mode, but it will allow Briefcase commands to run.
+
+### HTTP and HTTPS configuration
+
+Briefcase uses [httpx2](https://pydantic.dev/docs/httpx2/) to perform HTTP and HTTPS downloads. As a result, it honors the following six environment variables:
+
+#### Proxy configuration
+
+- `HTTP_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy server to use for HTTP requests.
+- `HTTPS_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy server to use for HTTPS requests.
+- `ALL_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy server to use when no protocol-specific proxy is configured.
+- `NO_PROXY` - A comma-separated list of hosts for which a proxy should not be used.
+
+#### SSL certificate configuration
+
+- `SSL_CERT_FILE` - The path to a file containing the trusted SSL certificates to use when verifying HTTPS connections.
+- `SSL_CERT_DIR` - The path to a directory containing trusted SSL certificates to use when verifying HTTPS connections.
+
+These variables are only used to configure the HTTP requests made by Briefcase; they do not affect the apps that Briefcase builds. Briefcase will report an error if a certificate path does not exist or is not the expected type, or if a proxy variable does not contain a valid proxy URL. For full details on how these variables are interpreted, see the [httpx2 environment variable documentation](https://pydantic.dev/docs/httpx2/environment_variables/).
